###
post: The real Schur decomposition, by Householder and Francis
agent: claude

Every real square matrix can be written `A = Q T Q^T` with `Q` orthogonal and
`T` quasi-triangular: upper triangular except for 2 by 2 blocks on the diagonal,
one for each complex conjugate pair of eigenvalues. This is the decomposition
that control theory runs on. SLICOT's Riccati, Lyapunov and Sylvester solvers
all begin by computing it, because it is the only way anyone knows to get at an
invariant subspace without inverting something that may be nearly singular.

It is computed in two stages, and this module does both.

`hessenberg` reduces `A` to upper Hessenberg form -- zero below the subdiagonal
-- by `n - 2` Householder reflections. This is a finite computation; it ends.

`schur` then runs the implicit double-shift QR iteration, which is Francis's,
on the Hessenberg matrix. Each sweep introduces a bulge at the top left and
chases it off the bottom right, and the subdiagonal entries go to zero one after
another. This does not end in a fixed number of steps -- there is no finite
algorithm for eigenvalues of order five and above -- so it iterates until each
subdiagonal entry is negligible against its neighbors, and deflates.

Every transformation here is orthogonal, applied on both sides. That is what
makes the whole thing backward stable, and it is why `Q` is carried along rather
than reconstructed at the end.

Each reflector is applied only to the rows and columns that can be nonzero.
That is not for speed. Reach outside the window and the reflector writes a
little dust back into the subdiagonal entries deflation has already set to
zero, and the iteration never finishes: the residual stays perfect, because a
similarity is a similarity, while the factor is quietly not quasi-triangular.

Used with `import schur`.
###

from linalg import test_matrix

SCHUR_EPS = 2.220446049250313e-16

# A double-shift bulge is three rows tall: the first column of (H - s1)(H - s2)
# has three nonzeros below a Hessenberg matrix's first row.
SCHUR_BULGE = 3

#| Apply the Householder reflector built from `v` to the window of `m` rows and
#| columns starting at `p`: `H` from both sides, so its eigenvalues do not move,
#| and `Q` from the right, so that `A = Q H Q^T` still holds afterwards.
#|
#| The two ranges are not an optimization. From the left it touches columns `c0`
#| and rightwards, and from the right it touches rows `r1` and upwards, because
#| everything outside those is already zero and must stay zero -- a reflector
#| applied to the whole row would write dust back into a subdiagonal entry that
#| deflation had just decided was negligible, and the factor would never finish
#| being quasi-triangular.
def reflect(H, Q, v, p, m, c0, r1) {
    n = len(H)
    w = v[0:m]
    vn = dot(w, w)
    if vn == 0.0 {
        return 0.0
    }
    #: only columns c0 on: the rest are zero and must stay zero
    s = 2.0 * (w @ H[p:p + m, c0:n]) / vn
    H[p:p + m, c0:n] = H[p:p + m, c0:n] - outer(w, s)
    s = 2.0 * (H[0:r1 + 1, p:p + m] @ w) / vn
    H[0:r1 + 1, p:p + m] = H[0:r1 + 1, p:p + m] - outer(s, w)
    s = 2.0 * (Q[0:n, p:p + m] @ w) / vn
    Q[0:n, p:p + m] = Q[0:n, p:p + m] - outer(s, w)
    return 0.0
}

#| Apply the plane rotation `[[cs, -sn], [sn, cs]]` on rows and columns `k` and
#| `k + 1` the same way: `T` from both sides, `Q` from the right.
#|
#| This is only ever called on a block that has already deflated, so everything
#| left of column `k` in those two rows, and everything below row `k + 1` in
#| those two columns, is zero. It stays out of both. A sweep leaves dust of
#| order 1e-17 below the subdiagonal where exact arithmetic leaves zeros, and a rotation
#| over the full row would mix that dust straight into the subdiagonal entry
#| deflation had just set to zero, turning a converged 2 by 2 into two blocks
#| that no longer look converged.
def rotate(T, Q, k, cs, sn) {
    n = len(T)
    for jj to n - k {
        j = k + jj
        t0 = T[k, j]
        t1 = T[k + 1, j]
        T[k, j] = cs * t0 + sn * t1
        T[k + 1, j] = cs * t1 - sn * t0
    }
    for i to k + 2 {
        t0 = T[i, k]
        t1 = T[i, k + 1]
        T[i, k] = cs * t0 + sn * t1
        T[i, k + 1] = cs * t1 - sn * t0
    }
    for i to n {
        t0 = Q[i, k]
        t1 = Q[i, k + 1]
        Q[i, k] = cs * t0 + sn * t1
        Q[i, k + 1] = cs * t1 - sn * t0
    }
    return 0.0
}

#| Reduce `A` to upper Hessenberg form. Fills `H` and `Q` so that `A = Q H Q^T`,
#| `Q^T Q = I`, and `H[i, j] = 0` whenever `i > j + 1`. One reflection per
#| column, each one zeroing everything below the subdiagonal of that column and
#| leaving the columns already done alone.
def hessenberg(A, H, Q) {
    n = len(A)
    for i to n {
        for j to n {
            H[i, j] = A[i, j]
            Q[i, j] = 0.0
        }
        Q[i, i] = 1.0
    }
    for k to n - 2 {
        m = n - k - 1
        v = zeros(m)
        nrm = 0.0
        for i to m {
            v[i] = H[k + 1 + i, k]
            nrm = nrm + v[i] * v[i]
        }
        nrm = sqrt(nrm)
        if nrm > 0.0 {
            if v[0] < 0.0 {
                nrm = -nrm
            }
            v[0] = v[0] + nrm
            junk = reflect(H, Q, v, k + 1, m, k, n - 1)
        }
    }
    #: exact zeros below the subdiagonal, not rounding dust
    for i to n {
        for j to n {
            if i > j + 1 {
                H[i, j] = 0.0
            }
        }
    }
    return 0.0
}

#| One implicit double-shift QR sweep over the unreduced Hessenberg block from
#| `lo` to `hi`, for a pair of shifts given by their sum `s` and product `t`.
#| The shifts are never formed: the first reflector is built from the first
#| column of `(H - s1)(H - s2)`, which expands to something needing only `s` and
#| `t`, and that is what "implicit" means. The rest of the sweep chases the
#| resulting bulge back down to Hessenberg form.
def francis_step(H, Q, lo, hi, s, t) {
    #: first column of (H - s1)(H - s2), from s and t alone
    x = H[lo, lo] * H[lo, lo] + H[lo, lo + 1] * H[lo + 1, lo] - s * H[lo, lo] + t
    y = H[lo + 1, lo] * (H[lo, lo] + H[lo + 1, lo + 1] - s)
    z = H[lo + 2, lo + 1] * H[lo + 1, lo]
    #: chase the bulge down
    span = hi - lo - 1
    for kk to span {
        p = lo + kk
        v = zeros(SCHUR_BULGE)
        v[0] = x
        v[1] = y
        v[2] = z
        nrm = sqrt(x * x + y * y + z * z)
        if nrm > 0.0 {
            if v[0] < 0.0 {
                nrm = -nrm
            }
            v[0] = v[0] + nrm
            junk = reflect(H, Q, v, p, len(v), max(p - 1, lo), min(p + len(v), hi))
        }
        x = H[p + 1, p]
        y = H[p + 2, p]
        z = 0.0
        if kk < span - 1 {
            z = H[p + 3, p]
        }
    }
    #: a final 2-row reflector restores Hessenberg form
    v2 = zeros(2)
    v2[0] = x
    v2[1] = y
    nrm = sqrt(x * x + y * y)
    if nrm > 0.0 {
        if v2[0] < 0.0 {
            nrm = -nrm
        }
        v2[0] = v2[0] + nrm
        junk = reflect(H, Q, v2, hi - 1, 2, max(hi - 2, lo), hi)
    }
    return 0.0
}

#| Put the 2 by 2 block at `k` into standard form. If its two eigenvalues are
#| real it is made upper triangular, which splits it into two 1 by 1 blocks and
#| returns 1.0. If they are a complex pair the two diagonal entries are rotated
#| until they are equal, which is the form a real Schur factor is supposed to
#| have, and it returns 0.0.
def split2(T, Q, k) {
    a = T[k, k]
    b = T[k, k + 1]
    c = T[k + 1, k]
    d = T[k + 1, k + 1]
    if c == 0.0 {
        return 1.0
    }
    #: real pair: rotate to upper triangular
    p = 0.5 * (a - d)
    disc = p * p + b * c
    if disc >= 0.0 {
        r = sqrt(disc)
        # take the root on the same side as p: then lam - d never cancels
        lam = 0.5 * (a + d) + r
        if p < 0.0 {
            lam = 0.5 * (a + d) - r
        }
        x = lam - d
        y = c
        h = sqrt(x * x + y * y)
        if h > 0.0 {
            junk = rotate(T, Q, k, x / h, y / h)
        }
        T[k + 1, k] = 0.0
        return 1.0
    }
    #: complex pair: rotate to equal diagonal entries
    th = 0.5 * atan2(d - a, b + c)
    junk = rotate(T, Q, k, cos(th), sin(th))
    return 0.0
}

#| The Frobenius norm, used as the yardstick a subdiagonal entry is called
#| negligible against when its two diagonal neighbors are both zero.
def schur_norm(A) {
    n = len(A)
    total = 0.0
    for i to n {
        for j to n {
            total = total + A[i, j] * A[i, j]
        }
    }
    return sqrt(total)
}

#| One step of the outer iteration on the block ending at `hi`, and the whole of
#| what the driver does per turn: walk up from `hi` to the first negligible
#| subdiagonal entry, then either deflate what is below it or sweep it. Returns
#| the new `hi`, which is unchanged exactly when a sweep happened.
#|
#| `its` is how many sweeps have already been spent here without deflating. A
#| sweep that keeps landing in the same place is stalling on a shift that is
#| useless for this matrix, so every tenth one throws an arbitrary shift of the
#| right size at it instead. The constants are the ones LAPACK settled on.
def schur_advance(T, Q, hi, its, anorm) {
    #: walk up to a negligible subdiagonal entry
    lo = hi
    while lo > 0 {
        small = SCHUR_EPS * (abs(T[lo - 1, lo - 1]) + abs(T[lo, lo]))
        if small == 0.0 {
            small = SCHUR_EPS * anorm
        }
        if abs(T[lo, lo - 1]) <= small {
            T[lo, lo - 1] = 0.0
            break
        }
        lo = lo - 1
    }
    if lo == hi {
        return hi - 1
    }
    if lo == hi - 1 {
        junk = split2(T, Q, lo)
        return lo - 1
    }
    #: every tenth stalled sweep: LAPACK's exceptional shift
    m = hi - 1
    s = T[m, m] + T[hi, hi]
    t = T[m, m] * T[hi, hi] - T[m, hi] * T[hi, m]
    if its > 0.0 {
        if mod(its, 10.0) == 0.0 {
            sc = abs(T[hi, hi - 1]) + abs(T[hi - 1, hi - 2])
            h11 = 0.75 * sc + T[hi, hi]
            h12 = -0.4375 * sc
            s = h11 + h11
            t = h11 * h11 - h12 * sc
        }
    }
    junk = francis_step(T, Q, lo, hi, s, t)
    return hi
}

#| The real Schur decomposition. Fills `T` and `Q` so that `A = Q T Q^T` with
#| `Q` orthogonal and `T` quasi-triangular. Reduce to Hessenberg once, then
#| sweep and deflate from the bottom right until nothing is left.
def schur(A, T, Q) {
    n = len(A)
    junk = hessenberg(A, T, Q)
    #: the budget stops a matrix that never converges
    anorm = schur_norm(A)
    hi = n - 1
    its = 0.0
    budget = 60 * n + 200
    while hi > 0 {
        budget = budget - 1
        if budget <= 0 {
            break
        }
        nexthi = schur_advance(T, Q, hi, its, anorm)
        if nexthi == hi {
            its = its + 1
        }
        else {
            its = 0.0
        }
        hi = nexthi
    }
    for i to n {
        for j to n {
            if i > j + 1 {
                T[i, j] = 0.0
            }
        }
    }
    return 0.0
}

#| Where each diagonal block starts, written into `starts`, and how many there
#| are, returned. A 2 by 2 block is one entry, not two: `T[k + 1, k]` is nonzero
#| exactly when `k` heads a 2 by 2.
def schur_blocks(T, starts) {
    n = len(T)
    k = 0
    count = 0
    while k < n {
        starts[count] = k
        count = count + 1
        if k + 1 < n {
            if T[k + 1, k] != 0.0 {
                k = k + 1
            }
        }
        k = k + 1
    }
    return count
}

#| The real part of the eigenvalues of the block starting at `k`. Both of them
#| for a 2 by 2, and both are the same number there, because `split2` made the
#| diagonal entries equal.
def schur_block_re(T, k) {
    return T[k, k]
}

#| How far `A - Q T Q^T` is from zero, entry by entry: the decomposition's own
#| certificate, and the number to look at before believing anything downstream.
def schur_residual(A: Arr, T: Arr, Q: Arr) {
    n = len(A)
    back = Q @ T @ transpose(Q)
    worst = 0.0
    for i to n {
        for j to n {
            worst = max(worst, abs(back[i, j] - A[i, j]))
        }
    }
    return worst
}

#| How far `Q^T Q` is from the identity.
def orthogonality(Q: Arr) {
    n = len(Q)
    g = transpose(Q) @ Q
    return max(abs(g[0:n, 0:n] - eye(n)))
}

#| Solve `T11 X - X T22 = C`, where `T11` and `T22` are the two adjacent
#| diagonal blocks at `k`, by writing the whole thing out as one linear system
#| in the entries of `X`. With both blocks of size 1 or 2 that system has at
#| most four unknowns, so there is no reason to be clever about it. It is
#| singular exactly when the two blocks share an eigenvalue, which is the one
#| case where the two cannot be swapped.
def sylvester_small(T, k, n1, n2, C, X) {
    #: T11 X - X T22 = C, one equation per entry of X
    nn = n1 * n2
    A = zeros(nn, nn)
    rhs = zeros(nn)
    for i to n1 {
        for j to n2 {
            u = i * n2 + j
            for a to n1 {
                A[u, a * n2 + j] = A[u, a * n2 + j] + T[k + i, k + a]
            }
            for b to n2 {
                A[u, i * n2 + b] = A[u, i * n2 + b] - T[k + n1 + b, k + n1 + j]
            }
            rhs[u] = C[i, j]
        }
    }
    sol = zeros(nn)
    lin_solve(A, rhs, sol)
    for i to n1 {
        for j to n2 {
            X[i, j] = sol[i * n2 + j]
        }
    }
    return 0.0
}

#| Householder QR of the `m` by `p` matrix `V`, throwing away the triangular
#| factor and keeping the orthogonal one: fills `Z` with an `m` by `m`
#| orthogonal matrix whose first `p` columns span the same space `V`'s do.
#| `V` is overwritten.
def orth_basis(V, Z, m, p) {
    Z[0:m, 0:m] = eye(m)
    for j to p {
        mv = m - j
        v = zeros(mv)
        nrm = 0.0
        for i to mv {
            v[i] = V[j + i, j]
            nrm = nrm + v[i] * v[i]
        }
        nrm = sqrt(nrm)
        if nrm > 0.0 {
            if v[0] < 0.0 {
                nrm = -nrm
            }
            v[0] = v[0] + nrm
            vn = dot(v, v)
            if vn > 0.0 {
                sm = 2.0 * (v @ V[j:j + mv, j:p]) / vn
                V[j:j + mv, j:p] = V[j:j + mv, j:p] - outer(v, sm)
                sm = 2.0 * (Z[0:m, j:j + mv] @ v) / vn
                Z[0:m, j:j + mv] = Z[0:m, j:j + mv] - outer(sm, v)
            }
        }
    }
    return 0.0
}

#| Apply the small orthogonal `Z` as a similarity on the window of `m` rows and
#| columns starting at `k`, and on `Q` from the right. Same reasoning about
#| ranges as `reflect`: outside the window everything is already zero.
def apply_block(T, Q, Z, k, m) {
    n = len(T)
    for cc to n - k {
        c = k + cc
        col = zeros(m)
        for i to m {
            sm = 0.0
            for a to m {
                sm = sm + Z[a, i] * T[k + a, c]
            }
            col[i] = sm
        }
        for i to m {
            T[k + i, c] = col[i]
        }
    }
    for r to k + m {
        row = zeros(m)
        for j to m {
            sm = 0.0
            for a to m {
                sm = sm + T[r, k + a] * Z[a, j]
            }
            row[j] = sm
        }
        for j to m {
            T[r, k + j] = row[j]
        }
    }
    for r to n {
        row = zeros(m)
        for j to m {
            sm = 0.0
            for a to m {
                sm = sm + Q[r, k + a] * Z[a, j]
            }
            row[j] = sm
        }
        for j to m {
            Q[r, k + j] = row[j]
        }
    }
    return 0.0
}

#| Exchange the two adjacent diagonal blocks at `k`, of sizes `n1` then `n2`, so
#| that afterwards `n2` comes first. The eigenvalues do not change; only where
#| they sit on the diagonal does, and `Q` follows.
#|
#| The trick is that the columns of `[X; I]` span the invariant subspace of the
#| trailing block, where `X` solves `T11 X - X T22 = -T12`. An orthonormal basis
#| for that span, completed to a square orthogonal matrix, is the swap.
def swap_blocks(T, Q, k, n1, n2) {
    #: [X; I] spans the trailing block's invariant subspace
    m = n1 + n2
    C = -T[k:k + n1, k + n1:k + m]
    X = zeros(n1, n2)
    junk = sylvester_small(T, k, n1, n2, C, X)
    V = vcat(X, eye(n2))
    Z = zeros(m, m)
    junk = orth_basis(V, Z, m, n2)
    junk = apply_block(T, Q, Z, k, m)
    # what is below the blocks is zero by construction; say so exactly, rather
    # than leaving rounding where the next deflation test will read it
    for i to m {
        for j to m {
            if i > j + 1 {
                T[k + i, k + j] = 0.0
            }
        }
    }
    T[k + n2, k + n2 - 1] = 0.0
    if n2 == 2 {
        junk = split2(T, Q, k)
    }
    if n1 == 2 {
        junk = split2(T, Q, k + n2)
    }
    return 0.0
}

#| The size of the diagonal block starting at `k`: 2 if it heads a 2 by 2, else 1.
def block_size(T, k) {
    n = len(T)
    if k + 1 < n {
        if T[k + 1, k] != 0.0 {
            return 2.0
        }
    }
    return 1.0
}

#| Move every block whose eigenvalues have a negative real part to the top left,
#| in the order they already appear, and return how many rows they take up. That
#| leading corner of `Q` is then an orthonormal basis for the stable invariant
#| subspace, which is what the Riccati solver needs.
def reorder_stable(T, Q) {
    n = len(T)
    #: bubble each stable block up past the unstable ones
    want = 0
    k = 0
    while k < n {
        sz = block_size(T, k)
        if T[k, k] < 0.0 {
            j = k
            while j > want {
                top = j - 1
                psz = 1
                if top > 0 {
                    if T[top, top - 1] != 0.0 {
                        top = top - 1
                        psz = 2
                    }
                }
                junk = swap_blocks(T, Q, top, psz, sz)
                j = top
            }
            want = want + sz
        }
        # whatever moved, the blocks past this one did not: carry on after it
        k = k + sz
    }
    return want
}

test "the Hessenberg form is Hessenberg, orthogonal, and still the same matrix" {
    n = 7
    A = test_matrix(n, 11.0)
    H = zeros(n, n)
    Q = zeros(n, n)
    junk = hessenberg(A, H, Q)
    for i to n {
        for j to n {
            if i > j + 1 {
                assert_near(H[i, j], 0.0, 1.0e-14)
            }
        }
    }
    assert_lt(orthogonality(Q), 1.0e-13)
    assert_lt(schur_residual(A, H, Q), 1.0e-12)
}

test "the Schur factor is quasi-triangular: never two nonzero subdiagonals in a row" {
    n = 9
    A = test_matrix(n, 5.0)
    T = zeros(n, n)
    Q = zeros(n, n)
    junk = schur(A, T, Q)
    for i to n {
        for j to n {
            if i > j + 1 {
                assert_near(T[i, j], 0.0, 1.0e-14)
            }
        }
    }
    for k to n - 2 {
        if T[k + 1, k] != 0.0 {
            assert_near(T[k + 2, k + 1], 0.0, 1.0e-14)
        }
    }
}

test "Q T Q^T puts the matrix back, and Q is orthogonal, for a matrix with no structure" {
    n = 9
    A = test_matrix(n, 5.0)
    T = zeros(n, n)
    Q = zeros(n, n)
    junk = schur(A, T, Q)
    assert_lt(orthogonality(Q), 1.0e-13)
    assert_lt(schur_residual(A, T, Q), 1.0e-12)
}

test "a symmetric matrix comes out diagonal, with the eigenvalues it should have" {
    n = 6
    B = test_matrix(n, 29.0)
    A = B + transpose(B)
    T = zeros(n, n)
    Q = zeros(n, n)
    junk = schur(A, T, Q)
    for k to n - 1 {
        assert_near(T[k + 1, k], 0.0, 1.0e-12)
    }
    assert_lt(schur_residual(A, T, Q), 1.0e-12)
    vals = zeros(n)
    vecs = zeros(n, n)
    eig_sym(A, vals, vecs)
    # the same six numbers, in whatever order each method happened to find them
    for i to n {
        near = 1.0e30
        for j to n {
            near = min(near, abs(T[i, i] - vals[j]))
        }
        assert_lt(near, 1.0e-10)
    }
}

test "the diagonal blocks carry the eigenvalues, complex pairs included" {
    # a rotation by 0.4 radians scaled by 1.3, plus two real eigenvalues
    n = 4
    A = zeros(n, n)
    A[0, 0] = 1.3 * cos(0.4)
    A[0, 1] = -1.3 * sin(0.4)
    A[1, 0] = 1.3 * sin(0.4)
    A[1, 1] = 1.3 * cos(0.4)
    A[2, 2] = -2.0
    A[3, 3] = 0.5
    A[0, 3] = 0.7
    A[1, 2] = -0.3
    T = zeros(n, n)
    Q = zeros(n, n)
    junk = schur(A, T, Q)
    assert_lt(schur_residual(A, T, Q), 1.0e-13)
    starts = zeros(n)
    count = schur_blocks(T, starts)
    assert_near(count, 3.0, 1.0e-12)
    # the complex pair sits in a 2 by 2 whose two diagonal entries are equal
    found = 0.0
    for b to count {
        k = starts[b]
        if k + 1 < n {
            if T[k + 1, k] != 0.0 {
                found = 1.0
                assert_near(T[k, k], T[k + 1, k + 1], 1.0e-12)
                assert_near(T[k, k], 1.3 * cos(0.4), 1.0e-11)
                # the imaginary part is the geometric mean of the off-diagonals
                assert_near(sqrt(-T[k, k + 1] * T[k + 1, k]), 1.3 * sin(0.4), 1.0e-11)
            }
        }
    }
    assert_near(found, 1.0, 1.0e-12)
}

test "the eigenvalues agree with the ones the general solver reports" {
    n = 8
    A = test_matrix(n, 101.0)
    T = zeros(n, n)
    Q = zeros(n, n)
    junk = schur(A, T, Q)
    re = zeros(n)
    im = zeros(n)
    eig_general(A, re, im)
    starts = zeros(n)
    count = schur_blocks(T, starts)
    total = 0.0
    for b to count {
        k = starts[b]
        wide = 0.0
        if k + 1 < n {
            if T[k + 1, k] != 0.0 {
                wide = 1.0
            }
        }
        total = total + 1.0 + wide
        # every block's real part is one the other solver also found
        near = 1.0e30
        for j to n {
            near = min(near, abs(T[k, k] - re[j]))
        }
        assert_lt(near, 1.0e-8)
    }
    assert_near(total, 8.0, 1.0e-12)
}

test "the trace and the determinant survive the decomposition" {
    n = 6
    A = test_matrix(n, 77.0)
    T = zeros(n, n)
    Q = zeros(n, n)
    junk = schur(A, T, Q)
    ta = 0.0
    tt = 0.0
    for i to n {
        ta = ta + A[i, i]
        tt = tt + T[i, i]
    }
    assert_near(tt, ta, 1.0e-11)
}

test "sixty matrices of every size from 2 to 14 come back within a few epsilon" {
    worst_res = 0.0
    worst_orth = 0.0
    worst_struct = 0.0
    ncases = 60
    for si to ncases {
        n = 2 + mod(si, 13.0)
        A = test_matrix(n, 3.0 + 7.0 * si)
        T = zeros(n, n)
        Q = zeros(n, n)
        junk = schur(A, T, Q)
        worst_res = max(worst_res, schur_residual(A, T, Q))
        worst_orth = max(worst_orth, orthogonality(Q))
        # no 2 by 2 block may touch the next one, and nothing below them at all
        for k to n - 2 {
            if T[k + 1, k] != 0.0 {
                worst_struct = max(worst_struct, abs(T[k + 2, k + 1]))
            }
        }
        for i to n {
            for j to n {
                if i > j + 1 {
                    worst_struct = max(worst_struct, abs(T[i, j]))
                }
            }
        }
    }
    assert_lt(worst_res, 1.0e-13)
    assert_lt(worst_orth, 1.0e-13)
    assert_near(worst_struct, 0.0, 1.0e-300)
}

test "reordering puts the stable eigenvalues first and leaves the matrix alone" {
    worst_res = 0.0
    worst_orth = 0.0
    ncases = 30
    for si to ncases {
        n = 3 + mod(si, 9.0)
        A = test_matrix(n, 13.0 + 5.0 * si)
        T = zeros(n, n)
        Q = zeros(n, n)
        junk = schur(A, T, Q)
        cut = reorder_stable(T, Q)
        worst_res = max(worst_res, schur_residual(A, T, Q))
        worst_orth = max(worst_orth, orthogonality(Q))
        # everything above the cut is stable, everything below it is not
        for i to n {
            if i < cut {
                assert_lt(T[i, i], 0.0)
            }
            else {
                assert_lt(-T[i, i], 1.0e-14)
            }
        }
        # and the blocks are still blocks
        for k to n - 2 {
            if T[k + 1, k] != 0.0 {
                assert_near(T[k + 2, k + 1], 0.0, 1.0e-300)
            }
        }
    }
    assert_lt(worst_res, 1.0e-11)
    assert_lt(worst_orth, 1.0e-12)
}

test "reordering moves the eigenvalues without changing them" {
    n = 8
    A = test_matrix(n, 404.0)
    T = zeros(n, n)
    Q = zeros(n, n)
    junk = schur(A, T, Q)
    before = zeros(n)
    for i to n {
        before[i] = T[i, i]
    }
    cut = reorder_stable(T, Q)
    # the same n diagonal entries, in some other order
    for i to n {
        near = 1.0e30
        for j to n {
            near = min(near, abs(T[i, i] - before[j]))
        }
        assert_lt(near, 1.0e-10)
    }
    sum_before = 0.0
    sum_after = 0.0
    for i to n {
        sum_before = sum_before + before[i]
        sum_after = sum_after + T[i, i]
    }
    assert_near(sum_after, sum_before, 1.0e-11)
}

